# NetAttackAI

**AI-driven, local-first penetration testing & bug bounty research agent.**

NetAttackAI is a local-first Python application that uses Ollama LLMs to plan and
execute authorized security assessments. It couples a defensive, scope-gated MCP
scanning server with a permissive, target-locked exploit MCP server, a multi-agent
swarm, an autonomous campaign orchestrator, a Textual TUI, and an interactive menu —
all driven by local models on the operator's own machine.

> **Status:** Beta (`0.49.2`) · **License:** GPL-3.0 · **Python:** 3.10–3.12

---

## ⚠️ Authorization & responsible use

NetAttackAI is an offensive security tool. **Run it only against networks, systems,
and applications you own or are explicitly authorized to test in writing.** Unauthorized
scanning or exploitation of systems you do not own or have no permission to test is
illegal and unethical.

By using this software you accept full responsibility for your actions. The authors
and contributors disclaim all liability for misuse. Treat the output as advisory and
verify every finding before acting on it. If in doubt, do not run it.

This is a **lab-only build**: the attack path is unrestricted but target-locked to a
single operator-specified IP (see [Safety & scope model](#safety--scope-model)). Recon
remains fully scope-gated and propose-only.

---

## What it is

NetAttackAI has two surfaces over the same core:

- **Exploitation engine** (`main.py` / `app.py`) — the modern, async, MCP-based path
  the operator actually runs. Opens an MCP exploit session, dispatches tool calls, and
  can drive a multi-agent swarm or a persistent autonomous campaign.
- **Legacy research loop** (`cli.py` + `agent_loop.py`) — a database-driven,
  scope-gated, headless/CI-friendly workflow using SQLite (`research_workspace/`).

Both share the same mission/scope schemas. Scope, command, and search safety are
enforced in Python code, not just in prompts.

## Features

- **MCP exploit engine** — a permissive exploit MCP server (`mcp_exploit_server.py`)
  exposing terminal execution, Python file write/run, searchsploit, Metasploit,
  msfvenom, impacket lateral movement, credential dumping, and more — gated at the
  policy layer.
- **Defensive MCP server** (`mcp_server.py`) — scope-gated Nmap scanning, sanitized
  vulnerability search, and NVD CVE lookup.
- **Multi-agent swarm** (`tools/swarm/`) — 6 specialist agents (recon, vuln, exploit,
  post-exploit, critic, reflection) with a shared blackboard and parallel dispatch.
- **Autonomous orchestrator** (`tools/autonomous_orchestrator.py`) — persistent
  multi-phase campaigns with adaptive aggression, auto-retry, and vulnerability chaining.
- **Textual TUI** (`python -m tui`) — 18-screen dashboard with missions, tasks, findings,
  evidence, scope, targets, target graph, reports, logs, settings, swarm, skills, and memory.
- **Interactive menu** — arrow-key-driven launcher (the default no-args behavior).
- **Runtime skills** — advisory prompt-context layer over a curated skill catalog, with
  semantic matching, mid-run re-selection, and cross-mission feedback.
- **Multi-model consultation** — optional advisory peer-model consultation when crafting
  exploits or recovering from repeated failures.
- **Long-session mode** — opt-in multi-hour attack runs with crash-safe resume.
- **Recon pipeline** — host discovery, service identification, enrichment, and
  recon-driven goal suggestion.
- **NVD CVE lookup** — circuit-breakered, rate-limited CVE intelligence.
- **Reporting** — per-run timelines, CVSS, vulnerability chains, and findings export.

## Architecture

A single assessment controller opens an MCP exploit session and dispatches tool calls
through a model router (Ollama). Recon flows through a defensive, scope-gated server;
attack flows through the permissive, target-locked exploit server. An optional swarm
decomposes work across specialist agents, and an autonomous orchestrator drives
persistent multi-phase campaigns. See [`docs/architecture.md`](docs/architecture.md)
and [`docs/runtime-flows.md`](docs/runtime-flows.md) for the full picture.

## Requirements

- **Python 3.10+** (3.10–3.12 supported).
- **nmap** on `PATH` (or set `nmap.path` in `config.yaml`). On Linux, `-O`/`-sS` need
  root — set `nmap.sudo: true` (uses `sudo -n`) or run as root; otherwise the defensive
  server auto-downgrades those flags.
- **Ollama** running at `http://localhost:11434` with the default model
  `glm-5.2:cloud` pulled (`ollama show glm-5.2:cloud`).
- **Optional Kali tooling** on Linux for the full arsenal: Metasploit (`msfconsole`),
  `searchsploit`, `tmux`, `hydra`, `impacket`. On Windows the attacker is Python-only.

## Installation

**Linux / macOS**

```bash
python3 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
python -m pip install -r requirements.txt
nmap --version                               # must be on PATH or set nmap.path
ollama show glm-5.2:cloud                    # default model — verify reachable
```

**Windows (PowerShell)**

```powershell
python -m venv .venv
.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
python -m pip install -r requirements.txt
nmap --version
ollama show glm-5.2:cloud
```

Convenience helpers:

```bash
make install          # venv + pip install -r requirements.txt
make doctor           # python main.py --doctor
./scripts/setup-linux.sh   # one-shot venv + deps + doctor check
```

For editable package metadata and dev extras (pytest, coverage, ruff):

```bash
python -m pip install -e ".[dev]"
```

## Quick start

```bash
python main.py                               # interactive menu (default no-args)
python main.py --menu                        # same interactive menu, explicit
python main.py --tui                         # launch the Textual TUI dashboard
python -m tui                                # same TUI, alternate entry

python main.py --target 10.0.0.50 --mode recon --recon-first
python main.py --target 10.0.0.50 --mode attack --goal backdoor
python main.py --target 10.0.0.50 --mode attack --swarm --critic --reflection --adaptive-exploits

python main.py --exploit --exploit-mode standalone --exploit-target 10.0.0.50 \
    --exploit-cve CVE-2021-44228 --exploit-permission full_access
```

Smoke checks:

```bash
python main.py --version                     # NetAttackAI 0.49.2
python main.py --doctor                      # environment + config diagnostics
python main.py --self-test                   # safe localhost smoke test
```

### Legacy research CLI (writes to `research_workspace/research.db`)

```bash
python cli.py init-mission --config mission.yaml
python cli.py next-task
python cli.py run-task T-00001
python cli.py list-findings
python cli.py generate-report F-00001
python cli.py status
```

## Configuration

All behavior-defining settings live in **`config.yaml`**. Mission scope (allowed /
disallowed assets, forbidden actions, risk profile, testing modes, rate limits) lives
in **`mission.yaml`**, loaded by `cli.py` / `mission.py`.

Important `config.yaml` defaults (lab build):

- `exploit.permission: full_access` — lab attack posture (recon still resolves to
  `read_only` via the missing-key fallback).
- `exploit.attack_mode: true`
- `exploit.require_explicit_allowlist: true` — the runtime `--target` is unioned in via
  `EXPLOIT_TARGET`; this is the target-IP lock.
- `exploit.allowed_targets: []`
- `swarm.enabled: true`
- `memory.semantic_enabled: true`
- `ollama.model: glm-5.2:cloud` (976K context)

Provider API keys are read from env vars referenced in `config.yaml`
(`NVD_API_KEY`, `OLLAMA_API_KEY`, `SERPAPI_API_KEY`); see [`.env.example`](.env.example).
A local key file (`secr.json`, gitignored) can be populated via
`python main.py --setup-api-keys`.

## CLI reference

| Flag | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `--target <ip>` | Target IP to recon or attack. |
| `--mode {recon,attack}` | `recon` = gather intel, `attack` = full exploitation. |
| `--goal <name>` | Preset goal (`backdoor`, `initial_access`, `privilege_escalation`, …). |
| `--custom-goal <text>` | Custom goal description. |
| `--recon-first` | Recon-then-attack flow. |
| `--tui` / `--menu` | Launch the TUI dashboard / force the interactive menu. |
| `--swarm` | Enable multi-agent swarm mode. |
| `--critic` / `--reflection` | Critic pre-approval / reflection agent (require `--swarm`). |
| `--adaptive-exploits` | Adaptive exploit selection. |
| `--long-session` | Opt-in multi-hour attack mode with crash-safe resume. |
| `--model <alias>` | Override default model alias (`glm`/`kimi`/`deepseek`/`deepseek_flash`/`minimax`). |
| `--mcp-transport {stdio,http}` | Exploit MCP transport. |
| `--skills {on,off,hints,lookup}` | Runtime skills mode. |
| `--multi-model-consult` | Enable advisory peer-model consultation. |
| `--exploit ...` | Standalone exploit subcommand (`--exploit-target`, `--exploit-cve`, `--exploit-permission`). |
| `--doctor` / `--self-test` | Environment diagnostics / safe localhost smoke test. |
| `--resume` | Resume from a prior session's checkpoint. |
| `--version` | Print version and exit. |

## Safety & scope model

- **Recon** is fully scope-gated and propose-only (`read_only`): the agent gathers intel
  and proposes attacks without executing them. A post-session `SafetyReviewer` runs on
  every tool call.
- **Attack** is **target-locked**: the AI may do whatever it takes to the one target IP,
  but cannot pivot to other hosts. The lock is enforced at the MCP tool layer by
  extracting every destination (URL authorities, `/dev/tcp` hosts, LHOST/RHOST, scanner
  targets, bare IPs) and refusing any not in the allowlist (`exploit.allowed_targets`
  ∪ the runtime `--target`).
- **Three permission levels** (`tools/exploit_agent/policy.py`): `full_access` (lab),
  `approve_only` (every action needs `ALLOW <ip>`), `read_only` (propose only).
- **Hard-forbidden actions** regardless of config (`scope_gate.py`): denial of service,
  destructive exploit, social engineering, physical attack, malware, credential theft.
- **Full JSONL audit trail** (`exploit_audit.jsonl`) with SHA256 of generated code.

See [`docs/safety-model.md`](docs/safety-model.md) for the full scope/risk/permission
model and secure-development rules.

## Project layout

```
main.py / app.py        assessment controller (Flow A entry points)
cli.py                  legacy research CLI (Flow B entry point)
mcp_server.py           defensive, scope-gated MCP server
mcp_exploit_server.py   permissive, target-locked exploit MCP server
config.yaml             runtime configuration
mission.yaml            mission scope + risk profile
tools/                  exploit agent, swarm, autonomous orchestrator, MCP tools,
                        recon pipeline, attack modules, skills, reporting, …
tui/                    Textual TUI (18 screens)
tests/                  ~80 test files (all mock subprocess / network)
docs/                   engineering docs (architecture, flows, safety, extension, …)
skills-to-add/          curated runtime skill catalog
```

## Testing

```bash
python -m pytest tests/ -v
python -m pytest tests/test_scope_gate.py -v                 # single file
python -m pytest tests/ -v -k "scope"                        # by keyword
```

Tests mock subprocess and network — no live Nmap fixtures. For safety-sensitive
changes also run `python main.py --doctor` and `python main.py --self-test`.
See [`docs/testing-guide.md`](docs/testing-guide.md).

## Documentation

Full engineering docs are in [`docs/`](docs/README.md): getting started, architecture,
runtime flows, module guide, extension guide, safety model, testing guide, and skills.

## Contributing

Contributions are welcome. Read [`docs/`](docs/README.md) first, add focused tests in
`tests/` for any change, and run `python -m pytest tests/ -v` before opening a PR.
Safety-sensitive changes need regression tests in `test_scope_gate.py`,
`test_safety_reviewer.py`, or `test_validation_utils.py`.

